left:54.52%;top:8.46%" title="Find the work">2</label><label class="pin p3" for="st-3" style="left:17.26%;top:46.15%" title="No plan yet: define, then plan">3</label><label class="pin p4" for="st-4" style="left:60.81%;top:6.92%" title="Work in a loop">4</label><label class="pin p5" for="st-5" style="left:60.81%;top:33.33%" title="Hooks keep the thread">5</label><label class="pin p6" for="st-6" style="left:70.00%;top:69.23%" title="Capture, then triage">6</label><label class="pin p7" for="st-7" style="left:1.94%;top:80.77%" title="The vault learns">7</label></div></figure>
<section class="steps" aria-labelledby="steps-title"><div class="steps__bar"><h2 id="steps-title">Read it in seven steps</h2><div class="chips"><label class="chip c1" for="st-1">1</label><label class="chip c2" for="st-2">2</label><label class="chip c3" for="st-3">3</label><label class="chip c4" for="st-4">4</label><label class="chip c5" for="st-5">5</label><label class="chip c6" for="st-6">6</label><label class="chip c7" for="st-7">7</label></div></div><p class="steps__hint">Press a number on the map, or one of these, to read its step.</p><article class="step s1"><span class="step__n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><div><h3>Enter through the desk.</h3><p>Every session, Claude Code on my machine or Claude in Cowork, reads <code>AGENTS.md</code> first: how to work with me, what never happens without my word, and where everything lives.</p><div class="step__nav"><label class="step__go" for="st-0">Close</label><label class="step__go step__go--next" for="st-2">Next →</label></div></div></article><article class="step s2"><span class="step__n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><div><h3>Find the work.</h3><p>The router, <code>PINNED.md</code>, lists the open plans. If the work has one, the agent goes straight to the office and opens its pointer, <code>now.md</code>, which says where the plan stands.</p><div class="step__nav"><label class="step__go" for="st-0">Close</label><label class="step__go" for="st-1">← Previous</label><label class="step__go step__go--next" for="st-3">Next →</label></div></div></article><article class="step s3"><span class="step__n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><div><h3>No plan yet: define, then plan.</h3><p><code>define-project</code> gathers context from the vault and interviews me, open questions first, then writes the project's cartridge. <code>planning</code> interviews me again and writes the plan, its pointer, its memory and its row on the router.</p><div class="step__nav"><label class="step__go" for="st-0">Close</label><label class="step__go" for="st-2">← Previous</label><label class="step__go step__go--next" for="st-4">Next →</label></div></div></article><article class="step s4"><span class="step__n" aria-hidden="true">4</span><div><h3>Work in a loop.</h3><p>Step by step, starting from the plan's pointer. In Claude Code, a guard run by a hook checks each command, write, web call, connector and subagent before it runs, and allows it, asks me or stops it; it does not watch reading files, and its known gaps are kept as tests. Then the checks run, each trusted only once a planted fault has fired it. Nothing is committed, merged, sent or published without my yes, given on the diff and on what the checks found; a gate keeps private data out of the public repository, and the turn is added to the record.</p><div class="step__nav"><label class="step__go" for="st-0">Close</label><label class="step__go" for="st-3">← Previous</label><label class="step__go step__go--next" for="st-5">Next →</label></div></div></article><article class="step s5"><span class="step__n" aria-hidden="true">5</span><div><h3>Hooks keep the thread.</h3><p>They run on events, not on the agent's choice. Before a compaction one saves the transcript; after it another puts the router back and has the agent call the note-taker, which keeps the plan's memory: decisions, changes of mind, lessons.</p><div class="step__nav"><label class="step__go" for="st-0">Close</label><label class="step__go" for="st-4">← Previous</label><label class="step__go step__go--next" for="st-6">Next →</label></div></div></article><article class="step s6"><span class="step__n" aria-hidden="true">6</span><div><h3>Capture, then triage.</h3><p>Loose ends from the work land in the inbox as one line each, without stopping it, and so do my own ideas to improve the system, written as they occur to me. Every Monday a reminder brings the weekly drain: <code>triage</code> sends each line to its destination with my yes. At a plan's close it distributes the plan's memory the same way.</p><div class="step__nav"><label class="step__go" for="st-0">Close</label><label class="step__go" for="st-5">← Previous</label><label class="step__go step__go--next" for="st-7">Next →</label></div></div></article><article class="step s7"><span class="step__n" aria-hidden="true">7</span><div><h3>The vault learns.</h3><p>Decisions, lessons and notes go back into NEXUS, my second brain, beside every course I have studied and every project's cartridge, and the next plan starts from them: the same mistake is not made twice.</p><div class="step__nav"><label class="step__go" for="st-0">Close</label><label class="step__go" for="st-6">← Previous</label></div></div></article></section></div>
